{"file":{"content":"The right way to proceed is not to try to prove the full quenched exclusion head-on. That statement is now essentially Collatz in finite-shadow form. Instead, split it into three smaller theorems/experiments, exactly as you listed:\n\n[\n\\text{(A) periodic words/cycles,}\n\\qquad\n\\text{(B) finite-word inverse theorem,}\n\\qquad\n\\text{(C) shadow-deviation forces descent certificates.}\n]\n\nOne practical note first: the uploaded `.tex` I can see still contains the older false formulation\n\n[\n\\text{“no Syracuse orbit has a non-stationary finite-level non-coboundary shadow.”}\n]\n\nIf your local copy has already been corrected, then the upload is stale. In the uploaded file, the section around `op:quenched` still needs the corrected “non-eventually-trivial / or forces descent” formulation.\n\n## 1. Piece A: classify shadow-rigid periodic words\n\nThis piece is actually provable now, and it is worth putting in the paper as a theorem. It gives a clean cycle-side strengthening.\n\nLet (w=(b_0,\\dots,b_{p-1})) be a periodic valuation word of period (p), and put\n\n[\nB=\\sum_{i=0}^{p-1}b_i.\n]\n\nThe accelerated Syracuse iterate has the affine form\n\n[\nT_w(x)\n======\n\n\\frac{3^p x+A_w}{2^B},\n]\n\nwhere\n\n[\nA_w\n===\n\n\\sum_{j=0}^{p-1}3^{p-1-j}2^{B_j},\n\\qquad\nB_j=\\sum_{i<j}b_i.\n]\n\nA periodic point realizing (w) must therefore satisfy\n\n[\nx=\\frac{A_w}{2^B-3^p}.\n]\n\nSo positivity requires\n\n[\n2^B>3^p,\n]\n\nand a positive integer cycle requires divisibility:\n\n[\n2^B-3^p\\mid A_w.\n]\n\nNow the two-point shadow average along the cycle is\n\n[\n\\frac1p\\sum_{i=0}^{p-1}\\chi'(2)^{-b_i}.\n]\n\nLet (z=\\chi'(2)). The stationary geometric value is\n\n[\nG(z)=\\sum_{b\\ge1}2^{-b}z^{-b}\n=============================\n\n\\frac{z^{-1}/2}{1-z^{-1}/2}.\n]\n\nThe theorem to prove is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{Every positive periodic orbit other than }1\\mapsto1\n\t\\text{ casts a non-coboundary finite shadow.}\n}\n]\n\nProof: if all non-coboundary letter characters matched the geometric value, then the same spreading argument used in the keystone theorem applies. The empirical distribution of (b_i) modulo (2\\cdot3^j) may differ from the geometric distribution only in the parity direction, and that parity correction is (O(3^{-j})) pointwise. Letting (j\\to\\infty), the finite periodic letter law would equal the infinite geometric law pointwise:\n\n[\n\\nu(b=k)=2^{-k}.\n]\n\nThat is impossible for a finite periodic word. Therefore some non-coboundary character detects the cycle.\n\nFor the trivial cycle, (b_i\\equiv2), and it is intentionally excluded.\n\nThis gives a useful clean theorem:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{nontrivial cycles are already ruled into the finite-shadow obstruction class.}\n}\n]\n\nIt does not eliminate cycles, but it says cycle elimination is exactly a special case of quenched shadow exclusion.\n\n## 2. Piece B: finite-word inverse theorem\n\nThis is the most important algebraic next step. Do not try to prove the full infinite-orbit statement at once. Prove a finite inverse theorem.\n\nFix (r), a non-coboundary character (\\chi'\\bmod3^r), and a word segment\n\n[\nb_0,\\dots,b_{N-1}.\n]\n\nLet\n\n[\nz=\\chi'(2).\n]\n\nThe shadow deviation is\n\n[\nD_N(\\chi')\n==========\n\n\\frac1N\\sum_{n<N}z^{-b_n}-G(z).\n]\n\nThe first finite inverse theorem is just Fourier inversion:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t|D_N(\\chi')|\\ge\\varepsilon\n\t\\Rightarrow\n\t\\text{the empirical distribution of }b_n\\bmod \\operatorname{ord}(z)\n\t\\text{ differs from geometric by }\\gg\\varepsilon.\n}\n]\n\nMore explicitly, if (m=\\operatorname{ord}(z)), then there exists a residue class (a\\bmod m) such that\n\n[\n\\left|\n\\frac1N#{n<N:b_n\\equiv a\\bmod m}\n--------------------------------\n\n\\sum_{\\substack{b\\ge1\\b\\equiv a\\bmod m}}2^{-b}\n\\right|\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\varepsilon}{m}.\n]\n\nThat is elementary but important: a nonstationary shadow is exactly a biased valuation-letter residue class.\n\nThe second finite inverse theorem should convert this into an edge-residue trap. Since\n\n[\nF_{\\chi'}(x_n,x_{n+1})\n======================\n\n# \\chi'(x_{n+1})\\overline{\\chi'(3x_n+1)}\n\nz^{-b_n},\n]\n\nthe same deviation means the empirical edge measure on\n\n[\nU_{3^r}\\times U_{3^r}\n]\n\ndiffers from the stationary edge law in a non-coboundary Fourier mode. By finite Fourier inversion, there is a set of directed residue edges\n\n[\nE\\subset U_{3^r}\\times U_{3^r}\n]\n\nsuch that\n\n[\n\\left|\n\\mu_N(E)-\\mu_{\\rm stat}(E)\n\\right|\n\\gg_{r}\\varepsilon.\n]\n\nSo you get:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{shadow bias}\n\t\\Rightarrow\n\t\\text{finite residue-edge trap.}\n}\n]\n\nThe third, harder inverse theorem is the real target:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{persistent residue-edge trap}\n\t\\Rightarrow\n\t\\text{descent or coboundary obstruction.}\n}\n]\n\nThis is not yet proved. But now it is concrete. You can state it as:\n\n**Finite inverse theorem for shadow bias.**\nFor every (r,\\chi',\\varepsilon) there exist (R,H) such that if a realizable Syracuse word segment of length (N\\gg H) has\n\n[\n|D_N(\\chi')|\\ge\\varepsilon,\n]\n\nthen either:\n\n[\n\\min_{0\\le h\\le H}x_{n+h}<x_n\n]\n\nfor some (n<N), or the segment is trapped in a finite algebraic edge subsystem modulo (3^R). The latter subsystem supports a coboundary character.\n\nYour coboundary classification then kills the second alternative for non-coboundary (\\chi').\n\nThat is the theorem which would start converting the finite-shadow machinery into a pointwise Collatz proof.\n\n## 3. How to attack B computationally\n\nFor each (r), enumerate long finite words or actual orbit windows and compute:\n\n[\nD_{I}(\\chi')\n============\n\n\\frac1{|I|}\\sum_{n\\in I}\n\\left(z^{-b_n}-G(z)\\right).\n]\n\nWhen (|D_I|) is large, record:\n\n1. the biased residue classes of (b_n\\bmod m);\n2. the corresponding edge classes ((x_n,x_{n+1})\\bmod3^r);\n3. whether the window contains a descent;\n4. whether the window has high height/critical-excursion structure;\n5. the smallest modulus (3^R) on which the edge trap becomes visible.\n\nThe conjectural pattern you want is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{large shadow bias windows coincide with forced descent or exit events.}\n}\n]\n\nIf that pattern is robust, then “shadow deviation forces descent” becomes plausible as a theorem.\n\n## 4. Piece C: rigidity certificates in long orbit prefixes\n\nThis is the most realistic route, because it avoids proving global normality. The theorem should not say:\n\n[\n\\text{all shadows converge on every nontrivial orbit.}\n]\n\nInstead it should say:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{persistent shadow deviation cannot be sustained without descent.}\n}\n]\n\nA good local statement is:\n\n**Shadow deviation forces descent.**\nFor every (r,\\chi',\\varepsilon), there exist (M,H) such that if an orbit segment\n\n[\nx_N,\\dots,x_{N+M}\n]\n\nsatisfies\n\n[\n\\left|\n\\frac1M\\sum_{n=N}^{N+M-1}\nF_{\\chi'}(x_n,x_{n+1})\n----------------------\n\nG(\\chi'(2))\n\\right|\n\\ge\\varepsilon,\n]\n\nthen there exists (0\\le h\\le H) with\n\n[\nx_{N+h}<x_N,\n]\n\nor at least\n\n[\n\\min_{N\\le n\\le N+M+H}x_n\n<\nx_N.\n]\n\nThis would be enough to prevent a bad divergent orbit from sustaining the keystone shadow.\n\nThe proof strategy should mimic your successful finite-side work:\n\n1. Shadow deviation gives a biased edge subsystem.\n2. The finite operator theorem says this subsystem is annealed-unstable.\n3. If the deterministic orbit remains inside it, the only way to avoid annealed mixing is to align with a finite obstruction.\n4. The coboundary classification says the only finite obstructions are (\\langle\\chi_2\\rangle).\n5. Non-coboundary bias therefore cannot persist.\n6. The escape from the biased subsystem corresponds to descent/exit.\n\nThe missing step is still deterministic:\n\n[\n\\text{annealed instability}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{deterministic escape}.\n]\n\nBut in the local descent version, this may be more accessible than global equidistribution.\n\n## 5. What can be proved immediately for C\n\nThere is a simple deterministic inequality that should be added.\n\nLet\n\n[\nS_N=\\sum_{n<N}(b_n-\\log_23).\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\n\\log_2 x_N\n==========\n\n## \\log_2 x_0\n\nS_N\n+\n\\sum_{n<N}\\log_2\\left(1+\\frac1{3x_n}\\right).\n]\n\nThus any long segment with\n\n[\n\\sum_{n=N}^{N+M-1}b_n\n\n>\n\nM\\log_23\n+\n\\log_2 x_N\n+\nO(\\log M)\n]\n\nforces descent somewhere in or after the segment.\n\nSo if a shadow deviation implies an excess of large (b_n)'s, descent is immediate. The hard case is the opposite: shadow deviation without positive valuation excess. That case is a purely residue-phase deviation and is the true remaining obstruction.\n\nTherefore split C into two cases:\n\n[\n\\textbf{C1: mean-visible shadow deviation.}\n]\n\nIf the deviation changes the mean letter value by (\\eta M), prove descent by the logarithmic identity.\n\n[\n\\textbf{C2: phase-only shadow deviation.}\n]\n\nIf the mean remains near critical but non-coboundary phase is biased, then use the finite inverse/residue-trap machinery.\n\nThis split will prevent the proof from treating easy drift cases as hard equidistribution cases.\n\n## 6. A more precise finite inverse target\n\nLet (\\nu_I) be the empirical law of (b_n) on a window (I). Decompose it into:\n\n[\n\\nu_I=\\nu_{\\rm geom}+\\delta_{\\rm mean}+\\delta_{\\rm phase}.\n]\n\nThe parity and mean directions are easy or exempt. The non-coboundary phase part is:\n\n[\n\\Pi_{\\rm nc}(\\nu_I-\\nu_{\\rm geom}).\n]\n\nThe local inverse theorem should say:\n\n[\n|\\Pi_{\\rm nc}(\\nu_I-\\nu_{\\rm geom})|\\ge\\varepsilon\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{residue trap modulo }3^R.\n]\n\nThen the descent theorem should say:\n\n[\n\\text{residue trap modulo }3^R\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{descent or exact coboundary}.\n]\n\nThis is the final finite algebra problem.\n\n## 7. What I would add to the paper now\n\nAdd a short section after the open problem titled:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Three finite approaches to quenched exclusion.}\n]\n\nState:\n\n1. **Periodic-word theorem** — provable now. Include the proof above.\n\n2. **Finite shadow inverse theorem** — formulated as a conjecture/theorem target:\n[\n\\text{bias}\\Rightarrow\\text{finite residue-edge trap}.\n]\n\n3. **Shadow deviation forces descent** — the most promising route:\n[\n\\text{persistent trap}\\Rightarrow\\text{descent unless coboundary}.\n]\n\nThis will make the final state constructive rather than just “we hit the wall.”\n\n## 8. Important warning\n\nThe final open problem remains essentially Collatz. The pieces above do not weaken it unless one of them is proved in a form that genuinely uses the finite-shadow structure to force descent. So avoid language like:\n\n[\n\\text{“only a finite inverse theorem remains.”}\n]\n\nBetter:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{A finite inverse theorem would be a new deterministic rigidity principle.}\n}\n]\n\nThat is honest and mathematically accurate.\n\n## 9. Concrete next computations\n\nRun three computational tracks.\n\n### Track A: periodic words\n\nEnumerate small periods (p), all words with\n\n[\n2^B>3^p\n]\n\nand divisibility\n\n[\n2^B-3^p\\mid A_w.\n]\n\nFor each hypothetical cycle word, compute the smallest (r) and (\\chi') detecting its shadow. This gives a table of cycle shadows.\n\n### Track B: finite windows\n\nFor large known orbits, scan windows for maximal non-coboundary shadow deviation:\n\n[\n\\max_{r\\le R,\\chi'} |D_I(\\chi')|.\n]\n\nFor each high-deviation window, record whether descent follows within (H) steps.\n\n### Track C: residue traps\n\nFor high-deviation windows, compute the anomalous edge sets modulo (3^r), then lift to (3^{r+1},3^{r+2}). Test whether the trap persists or dissolves. A persistent trap should either become coboundary-like or correlate with descent.\n\nThe desired empirical theorem is:\n\n[\n\\text{non-coboundary traps do not lift coherently without descent.}\n]\n\n## 10. Bottom line\n\nYou can now prove Piece A. Pieces B and C are the serious remaining research program.\n\nThe most realistic path is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{shadow deviation forces descent}\n}\n]\n\nnot full quenched equidistribution.\n\nThe proof should proceed by:\n\n[\n\\text{shadow bias}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{finite residue-edge trap}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{attempted coherent lift}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{coboundary or descent}.\n]\n\nThat is the sharpest remaining route. It is still a new deterministic rigidity theorem, but it is now formulated in the smallest finite language your program has produced.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_30.md","numLines":536,"startLine":1,"totalLines":536},"type":"text"}